      "title": "The monthly benefit",
      "narration": "Rosa earns 6,000 dollars a month. Her disability policy pays a stated monthly indemnity, a flat amount chosen at purchase rather than a reimbursement of bills. Insurers cap the benefit at a percentage of earned income, commonly 60 to 70 percent, so that disability never pays better than work; because individually paid benefits are tax-free, 60 percent of gross often approaches take-home pay. Rosa chooses 3,600 dollars. The benefit is fixed unless a rider adjusts it, which is why the riders in Lesson 31 exist. On the exam, the monthly benefit is the amount, the definition decides whether it is owed.",
