      "title": "The elimination period",
      "narration": "The elimination period is the time deductible: the number of days after disability begins before benefits start. Common choices are 30, 60, 90, 180 or 365 days. A longer elimination period lowers the premium because short disabilities never produce a claim. Rosa picks 90 days, planning to live on savings and sick pay in the meantime. Benefits are then paid monthly in arrears, so her first check arrives about a month after day 90, roughly four months after she stopped work. Some policies apply a shorter elimination period to accidents than to sickness.",
